<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Intolerable Acts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Coercive Acts</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Intolerable Acts, called the Coercive Acts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, were four punitive laws passed by the British Parliament in 1774 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a>. They closed the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, curtailed self-government in Massachusetts and allowed the quartering of troops, and they provoked the colonies to coordinate their resistance through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts.jpg" alt="Intolerable Acts" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Trumbull · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Declaration_of_Independence_(1819),_by_John_Trumbull.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Relations between the Thirteen Colonies and Parliament worsened after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> ended in 1763. The war had left the British government deep in debt, and Parliament enacted measures such as the Stamp Act of 1765 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/townshend-acts">Townshend Acts</a> of 1767 to raise revenue from the colonies. Protests led to the repeal of both, but the Declaratory Act of 1766 asserted Parliament's right to legislate for the colonies in all cases whatsoever.</p><p>On 16 December 1773 members of the Sons of Liberty destroyed 342 chests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> in protest at the Tea Act, which had given the British East India Company a monopoly on tea sales. News of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in January 1774. Parliament responded with four laws.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The Boston Port Act of 31 March 1774 closed the port until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> was paid for and the king was satisfied that order had been restored. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massachusetts-government-act">Massachusetts Government Act</a> of 20 May took away the colony's charter and made almost all government posts appointments of the governor, Parliament or the king, while limiting town meetings to one a year unless the governor called one.</p><p>The Administration of Justice Act of the same day let the governor send trials of accused royal officials to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> or elsewhere in the empire if he thought they could not get a fair trial in Massachusetts. The Quartering Act, which applied to all the colonies, allowed a governor to house soldiers in other buildings if suitable quarters were not provided. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec-act">Quebec Act</a>, passed in the same session but unrelated, enlarged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> into the Ohio Country and guaranteed free practice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, and colonists counted it among the Intolerable Acts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Parliament hoped to isolate radicals in Massachusetts, but the effect was the opposite. Colonists saw the acts as a threat to the liberties of all British America, and the harshness of some measures made it hard for moderates to speak for Parliament. Committees of correspondence sent delegates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> in September 1774. It created the Continental Association, a boycott of British goods, and pledged to support Massachusetts if it were attacked. The Revolutionary War began at Lexington and Concord in April 1775. The name Intolerable Acts did not arise until the nineteenth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Intolerable Acts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Boston Tea Party of 1773 led to the punitive <b>Coercive Acts</b>, and the colonies coordinated their resistance through the First Continental Congress in 1774.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Coercive Acts</b> of 1774, the First Continental Congress organised a boycott and petitioned the king.</blockquote><a href="/work/united-states-declaration-of-independence">United States Declaration of Independence · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Congress hoped the sanctions would pressure Parliament to address the colonies' grievances, especially by repealing the <b>Intolerable Acts</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-20">20 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They had gathered after the British blockaded Boston Harbor and Parliament passed the <b>Intolerable Acts</b> in answer to the Boston Tea Party.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-26">26 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Delegates from twelve colonies gathered at Carpenters' Hall to coordinate resistance to Britain's <b>Intolerable Acts</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-5">5 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
